  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 326 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nSaturday through Thursday:\n\nThe stalled frontal boundary is expected to get pushed to the\nsouthwest of Wisconsin on Saturday as the upper ridge lifts into\nMN and a more amplified trough develops over the eastern Great\nLakes. The frontal boundary may trigger a few showers and storms\n(similar location as a lake breeze) midday through evening \nSaturday so we are carrying chance pops. \n\nWith northeast to easterly winds across all of southern WI on \nSunday and the frontal boundary shunted to our west, we have a\ndry forecast on Sunday and Monday. Highs will be much lower \nthan previous days, ranging from the upper 70s east to the lower\n80s west on this holiday weekend. \n\nOur precip chances will return to southern WI Tuesday as the\nwarm and humid air returns due to low pressure tracking from the\nNorthern Plains into Ontario. This will be another situation \nwhere a frontal boundary could stall over southern WI and be the\nfocus for continuous chances for showers/storms through the \nrest of the work week. \n\nCronce",
